WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.610
Poorly acted scenes in motion pictures aren't
hard to find.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:07.500
Questionable acting chops demonstrated by
television stars, on the other hand, can get

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.080
buried a little deeper among the thousands
of hours of TV broadcast every day.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.660
But fear not, fans of the small screen — we've
screened more than 50 years of TV history

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.670
to put together this retrospective of cringe-worthy
clips.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.660
We guarantee your gratification from these
painfully poor performances.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.830
A frightful fight

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.630
Legendary television screenwriter and producer
Gene Roddenberry

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:33.880
created one of the most memorable shows in
history with the futuristic science fiction

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.460
series Star Trek.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.700
Unfortunately, lead actor William Shatner,
playing USS Enterprise Captain James T. Kirk,

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.760
often boldly went where no bad acting had
ever gone before.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.160
(Still better than Star Trek: Into Darkness)

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.940
Shatner may be a sci-fi icon, but he set acting
back eons with this painful-to-watch fight

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:57.600
sequence.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.340
(At least it's not Star Trek V: The Final Frontier)

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.440
Even famed American astrophysicist Neil deGrasse
Tyson weighed in on what's commonly referred

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.689
to as the "worst fight scene ever."

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:16.600
"I thought even by then, they could have been
more creative with what they imagine an alien

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.600
to look like."

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.280
Fortunately, this scene wasn't the space explorers'
final frontier — the original series aired

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.880
for three seasons, spinning off into an entire
multi-million dollar franchise, proving that

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.009
Star Trek wasn't just here today...

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:30.420
Gorn tomorrow.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.260
"Oh, not again!"

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.400
(A better use of Captain Kirk than Star Trek Generations)

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.999
The nose knows bad acting

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.689
Sticking with fight scenes that leave you
feeling queasy, martial arts maverick Chuck

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:49.520
Norris was known for dialing up some stomach-curdling
doozies on his 90's CBS show Walker, Texas

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:50.520
Ranger.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.990
None were more unappetizing than this front
porch frenzy.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.529
"But you are going to jail."

00:01:54.529 --> 00:01:55.529
"No way!

00:01:55.529 --> 00:01:57.780
No more jail, NEVER!"

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.479
A show known for its awful brawls, all of
those bouts surely bow down to this disaster.

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:08.940
Chuck Norris jokes aside, horrendous dialogue
and tone-deaf acting often turned his eight-season

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.750
long primetime drama into pure comedy gold.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.850
The bad guys in this fight scene will testify
to it.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.600
"Let me see...."

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.240
"You… you broke my nose!"

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.300
"I'll fix it."

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.560
"Ohhhh!!"

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.520
Baby on board

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.630
Talking about painful, there's no other way
to describe the tragically lifeless first

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:34.690
foray into acting by Bristol Palin, daughter
of former Alaska governor Sarah Palin.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.530
If you look closely, you might even see her
cue cards.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.760
"I couldn't find the school… eh heh."

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.320
"I came by yesterday morning at 9 to go with
you."

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.800
"Oh, I was out by 7…"

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.120
"I left you a note on the door last night,
but I see it's still there."

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.710
Two years after her own teen pregnancy saga
was revealed, Bristol got the acting bug.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:57.350
She appeared in an episode of ABC Family's
series The Secret Life of the American Teenager.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.670
"It's for teen moms."

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.560
"So, I didn't get here on my own?"

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:02.560
"Of course you did."

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.640
"You're the world's greatest French horn player!

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.650
And I'm Yo-Yo Ma!"

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:09.590
Palin's awkward cameo apparently was a one-time
acting gig; as she told E! Online,

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:10.590
"I'm not an actress.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.450
I'll leave that up to the experts, but I had
a great time here.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.870
I don't think I'll be doing any more acting
in the future."

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.879
Good idea.

00:03:17.879 --> 00:03:19.600
Good night sweet kitty

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.530
In the next scene, it's not even humans doing
the dirty work.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:28.010
This time, ferocious felines took their faulty
starring turn on an episode of NBC's short-lived

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.980
1983 series Manimal.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.080
Just like these poorly-edited cats, viewers
turned tail and ran… to another channel.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.670
Television audiences didn't bite, and Manimal
was one of the most infamous TV flops of the

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:56.290
decade.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:57.670
Powerless punch

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:02.600
Bad acting, racially insensitive plotlines,
and largely uninspired fight sequences gave

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:07.380
critics enough ammo to give Iron Fist's first
season a resounding thumbs down.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.680
Game of Thrones veteran Finn Jones played
Iron Fist's lead, the conflicted martial arts

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.350
expert Danny Rand.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.000
"Go slow… at the beginning."

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.620
(And act bad... for the whole season)

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.860
Critics said the overacting sensei was plagued
with cheesy lines and clueless, slow-paced

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:28.210
martial arts moves.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:32.460
Despite abysmal reviews for its first run,
loyal Marvel fans will get more of the man

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.980
with the glowing right hand, as Iron Fist
started filming season two in December 2017 (unfortunately).

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:40.240
Another Marvel misstep

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.210
Originally intended for a late 2018 movie
release, Marvel's Inhumans franchise was instead

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.830
hastily pushed into television production
in 2016.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:53.490
The last minute rush to air reflected in the
show's boring sets and bland lack of character

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:54.000
development.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:58.900
"You know this was originally was going to be a horrible movie instead of a horrible TV show, right?"

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.180
"Really?

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.490
You didn't WANT to be king, you just HAD to
be king."

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:08.400
It's clear here acting throughout the series
suffered, too, especially courtesy of lead

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.919
character Black Bolt.

00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:14.169
Since Black Bolt is mute and only communicates
with abbreviated sign language, head nods,

00:05:14.169 --> 00:05:19.020
and eye rolls, dialogue in the show is plagued
with uncomfortably long silent pauses.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.770
The Daily Beast summed up Inhumans succinctly,
writing,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:27.229
"This is, simply put, the worst thing to debut
on TV this fall and the worst disaster that

00:05:27.229 --> 00:05:29.300
merry Marvel has managed to cook up."

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.150
We can think of worse Marvel things but…
close enough.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.409
She's so excited

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:38.280
Whether giving us silver screen sludge in
Showgirls, or overacting away on the small

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:43.560
screen, Saved by the Bell veteran Elizabeth
Berkley has proven she's a princess of pitiful

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:44.639
performances.

00:05:44.639 --> 00:05:49.229
Playing stressed-out straight-A student Jessie
Spano, Berkley wore her crown with pride.

00:05:49.229 --> 00:05:51.420
"I'm so excited!

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.460
I'm so… scared!"

00:05:55.289 --> 00:05:59.280
The much-mocked scene stands out as the most
memorable in Saved by the Bell's run, yet

00:05:59.289 --> 00:06:01.360
it was almost left on the cutting room floor.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.150
The original script called for Jessie to actually
get addicted to speed, but NBC execs wouldn't

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.550
allow mentioning the drug.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:11.780
The network eventually okayed Spano's storyline
to switch from an addiction to speed to a

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:16.800
caffeine pill addiction, and "Jessie's Song",
as well as Berkeley's infamous acting, lived

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:17.819
on.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:21.320
"I'm so excited, I'm so excited!

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.740
I'm so… scared!"

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.040
Stick to the dancing

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.689
Sometimes, acting is so ridiculously over
the top that it actually becomes good again.

00:06:32.689 --> 00:06:33.689
"How much is it?"

00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:34.380
"It'll be $18."

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.660
"Here you go, keep the change!"

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:36.660
"Hi, doggie!"

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.800
We present the case of Alfonso Ribeiro.

00:06:38.809 --> 00:06:42.849
If you tap-danced on Broadway as a child,
and then moonwalked alongside the legendary

00:06:42.849 --> 00:06:47.189
Michael Jackson in a famous Pepsi commercial,
it's safe to say you can hold your own as

00:06:47.189 --> 00:06:48.599
an accomplished dancer.

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:52.430
But when Ribeiro made his transition into
television acting, some critics said he had

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:53.509
two left feet.

00:06:53.509 --> 00:06:55.369
"Carlton, man, listen.

00:06:55.369 --> 00:06:58.740
My date with Lisa didn't quite turn out the
way I planned."

00:06:58.740 --> 00:06:59.740
"Pray, tell.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:00.760
Why not?"

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:06.479
Ribeiro landed his signature TV role as Carlton
Banks in 1990 on NBC's The Fresh Prince of

00:07:06.479 --> 00:07:07.529
Bel-Air.

00:07:07.529 --> 00:07:11.539
Carlton had his share of funny moments — but
in this scene, Ribeiro took Carlton's character

00:07:11.539 --> 00:07:14.279
over the top like few actors ever have.

00:07:14.279 --> 00:07:16.439
"Oh God!

00:07:16.439 --> 00:07:19.689
No, no, no!"

00:07:19.689 --> 00:07:24.949
Luckily for television viewers, Ribeiro returned
to his dancing roots and his hilarious "Carlton"

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:27.880
moves, winning the 19th season of Dancing
with the Stars.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:29.960
But really, it doesn't get better than this.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.700
Thanks for watching!

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:42.939
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:42.939 --> 00:07:45.240
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

